<p>Three more members of Iran’s women’s football delegation in Australia have withdrawn their bids to remain in the country, leaving only three of the original seven still planning to stay as of Sunday. Australian authorities had granted the group humanitarian visas after the players said they feared persecution if they returned to Iran.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/SiDJpQkAPrdtnJmSIxXbW4Xx01VZQgRwodywt2Tyb3MYRlrnG83JRSSwfEuztBC2bAMPXrUrncP6yerlATYjTKgUeeJctRuwzamJBkM53TLFvzBOFdQvp9yGiHGNaJ4pHTdzGTx8ytx2CN9kGt-vvLjp0LhziEFWAHkPpk8VuLTdV21gQxGkYzGgcOaJg9MtX2rJvA2fNoi0sk839rxzLs6dotAwRse7' alt='Members of the Iranian women’s national soccer team before a match in Australia this month.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Embassy breach: Security concerns grew after a teammate who later chose to return home contacted the Iranian embassy and revealed where the group was staying.</li><li>Regional tension: One report said the team was in Australia for the Women’s Asian Cup when conflict involving Iran, Israel and the United States began.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Australian government: Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke said officials gave the players several options and stressed that while Canberra could offer safety and opportunity, it could not remove the pressure surrounding their decisions. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/three-iranian-women-soccer-players-return-home-after-seeking-asylum-australia'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Iranian state media: State media in Iran called members of the delegation &#x27;traitors&#x27; after they declined to sing the national anthem before a match, according to multiple reports. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2026/3/14/two-more-iranian-women-footballers-pull-australia-asylum-bids-state-media'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c98gz372683o'>Three more Iran football team members change minds over asylum</a> - bbc.com</li><li>